 with rows [-1,-5,4], [-10,-5,6], [3,3,-4]. This is exactly original option 3 and the stored answer. This is a source-backed derived judgment from the authentic solution; the Incorrect grade concerns submitted option 4, not this reviewed answer."}] "usage" {"q-91092" [] "e-8060" [] "q-91166" [] "e-8054" [] "e-8005" []} "basis" 1884 "retractions" [[17592186120473 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -2 & 12 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{14}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120473 :question/answer-fields 17592186119312] [17592186120472 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186120472 :question/answer-fields 17592186119306] [17592186119614 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below $a_{11}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186119614 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1}; R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-4)R_{1}; R_{4}:=R_{4} + 3R_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st column, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{1 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 & -9 \\\\ -10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ 9 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119615 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Find a $3\\times 3$ determinant that has the same value.\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186119615 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of $a_{23}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{1}:=C_{1} + C_{3}; C_{2}:=C_{2} + (-2)C_{3}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + 3C_{3}; =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = -\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -10 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186119611 :question/problem "Calculate the value of\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of $a_{31}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4 \\times 4$ determinant to a $3 \\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186119611 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of $a_{31}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (or column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\mid C_{2}:=C_{2} + 2C_{1}; C_{3}:=C_{3} + 3C_{1}; C_{4}:=C_{4} + (-3)C_{1}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot (-1)^{3 + 1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -5 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{vmatrix}; = 4(11 - 3); = 32\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186119307 17592186119308 17592186119309 17592186119310 17592186119311 17592186119313 17592186119314 17592186119315 17592186119316 17592186119317] "immutable_fields" [[17592186119306 :answer-field/correct] [17592186119306 :answer-field/id] [17592186119306 :answer-field/key] [17592186119306 :answer-field/type] [17592186119312 :answer-field/correct] [17592186119312 :answer-field/id] [17592186119312 :answer-field/key] [17592186119312 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
